<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    NphiesCore — App Privacy Manifest.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS EVEN THOUGH DISTRIBUTION IS INTERNAL TESTFLIGHT.
    Internal TestFlight testing skips Beta App Review, so nobody reads the
    listing or the nutrition label yet. The privacy manifest is NOT part of
    review — it is enforced by the automated check that runs at UPLOAD, before
    a build is ever distributed to anyone. A missing or contradicted manifest
    comes back as an ITMS-9105x email and the build is not processed. So this
    is required now, not at public launch.

    IT IS REGISTERED IN THE BUILD, NOT JUST ON DISK. A .xcprivacy that is not a
    member of the target's Copy Bundle Resources phase is invisible to the
    upload check and to Xcode's privacy report — the failure looks exactly like
    having no manifest at all. This one is wired in project.pbxproj as
    AC1DB0A5E9FC4B1D8E2F0001 (file reference, in the App group) and
    AC1DB0A5E9FC4B1D8E2F0002 (build file, in the Resources phase). Verify after
    any `npx cap sync ios` with:

        /usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c Print \
          "$(xcodebuild -showBuildSettings -project ios/App/App.xcodeproj \
             -target App 2>/dev/null | awk -F' = ' '/ BUILT_PRODUCTS_DIR/{print $2}')"/App.app/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy

    ── NSPrivacyTracking = false ────────────────────────────────────────────
    "Tracking" has Apple's narrow meaning: linking this app's data with data
    from other companies' apps, websites or data brokers, for advertising or
    ad measurement. Nothing here does that. The app talks to exactly one host
    — the project's own Supabase instance — and ships no advertising, no
    attribution and no analytics SDK. Grepping src/, package.json, index.html
    and vite.config.ts for analytics/telemetry/sentry/crashlytics/amplitude/
    mixpanel/posthog/gtag/firebase returns nothing but unrelated Arabic and
    English locale strings. NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is therefore empty, which
    is the only value consistent with tracking = false.

    ── NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY ──────────────────────
    This is the entry most likely to be "fixed" by someone assuming a
    Capacitor app must declare NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults
    (CA92.1). On Capacitor 8 it must not, and the reason is specific.

      * The app target's only native source is App/AppDelegate.swift. It is
        the stock Capacitor delegate — window, lifecycle stubs, and two
        forwards into ApplicationDelegateProxy. No required-reason API.

      * Four plugins are compiled FROM SOURCE into this target via
        ios/App/CapApp-SPM/Package.swift: @capacitor/app, @capacitor/keyboard,
        @capacitor/splash-screen, @capacitor/status-bar. Their combined iOS
        sources (five Swift files, two Objective-C files) contain no match for
        UserDefaults, systemUptime, mach_absolute_time, statfs,
        volumeAvailableCapacity, systemFreeSize, activeInputModes,
        creationDate, modificationDate, attributesOfItem, getattrlist, or the
        NSFile{Creation,Modification}Date keys. They ship no .xcprivacy of
        their own precisely because they have nothing to declare.

      * Capacitor and Cordova arrive as prebuilt, signed XCFrameworks from
        capacitor-swift-pm (pinned 8.4.2 in Package.resolved). Each carries
        its own PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy declaring an EMPTY
        NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes, and `nm -u` plus `strings` over
        Capacitor.framework's arm64 binary find no reference to NSUserDefaults,
        statfs, uptime, activeInputModes or getattrlist. A framework's manifest
        covers the framework; it is not inherited by, and does not substitute
        for, this file.

      * The reason a Capacitor 5/6/7 app WOULD have needed CA92.1 and this one
        does not: Capacitor 8 moved AppUUID and the persisted serverBasePath
        off UserDefaults onto a file-backed store —
        Capacitor/KeyValueStore.swift:177, `KeyValueStore.standard` is
        `.persistent(suiteName: "standard")`, which writes under
        FileManager's .libraryDirectory. Nothing calls NSUserDefaults anymore.

      * The app's own persistence — the auth session, the offline outbox, the
        entry cache — is localStorage and IndexedDB inside the WKWebView. That
        is WebKit's storage, implemented in a system framework. It is not this
        app calling NSUserDefaults, and it does not create a declaration duty.

    RE-CHECK THIS ON EVERY CAPACITOR MAJOR AND ON EVERY NEW PLUGIN. The list
    above is a claim about a specific dependency set, not a permanent property
    of the app. If a future upload returns ITMS-91053 naming a category, the
    correct response is to find the call and add the category with the reason
    code Apple's message quotes — never to add a category speculatively. An
    over-declaration is a false statement about the app in the same way an
    omission is.

    ── NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes ──────────────────────────────────────────
    Everything below leaves the device for the project's own Supabase backend,
    which is what makes it "collected" in Apple's sense even though there is no
    third party anywhere in the path. All of it is linked to the signed-in
    identity — the app is invite-only and every screen past sign-in is gated,
    so there is no anonymous mode to be unlinked in. None of it is used for
    tracking. The single purpose is App Functionality: this is the record of
    work the app exists to keep.

    NOT DECLARED, each for a checkable reason:
      * Device ID. Web Push would store a per-installation endpoint, but
        src/lib/push.ts:101 returns 'unsupported' when env.isNative, so a
        native build never subscribes and never creates one. If push is ever
        wired natively (APNs via a real plugin), Device ID must be added here.
      * Product Interaction / Analytics / Crash / Performance data. No
        analytics or crash SDK is present; see the grep noted above.
      * Health or sensitive personal data. Entries are programme and
        operations records — actions, decisions, issues, SLAs. If the app ever
        carries claim-level or patient-level data, this manifest and the App
        Store Connect nutrition label must both change before that ships.

    The App Store Connect privacy questionnaire must be answered to match this
    file exactly. The two are checked against each other.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Email address. The only sign-in credential: supabase.auth
		     signInWithOtp / verifyOtp / signInWithPassword in src/store/auth.ts
		     (:161, :176, :250). It is the account key and is stored in
		     auth.users. Admin-provisioned members get a synthetic
		     @opstrack.internal address, which is still an email address. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Name. public.profiles.display_name (migration 0001, :23) is the
		     person's own name, shown on every row they own. entries.owner_name
		     and entries.requester (:313, :314) are free-text names of other
		     people — a vendor contact, a colleague outside the tool — typed by
		     the user as part of the work record. Both are names, so both are
		     covered here rather than pretending only the account holder's is
		     collected. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- User ID. public.profiles.id, the auth.users uuid. It is the
		     foreign key on every row the app writes — ownership, authorship,
		     notifications, row-level security. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Other user content. The substance of the app: entry titles and
		     descriptions, tags and links (migration 0001, :304-:318), meeting
		     minutes, and the free text captured through the parser. Apple's
		     "Other User Content" is the correct bucket — it is neither
		     messaging between users nor uploaded media. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
